<Layout title="About" subtitle="What this site is, and how to check it.">
  <p>
    <strong>Dharmalekha</strong> — <em>dhamma</em> + <em>lekhā</em>, dharma
    writing. It publishes freely, and it is built so that you never have to take
    its word for anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is speaking</h2>

  <p>
    Every passage carries a rule in the left margin, and the rule tells you where
    the words came from before you read them.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>solid brass rule</strong> is canonical text. It is pasted from
    SuttaCentral, in a named translator's words, with a reference you can follow.
    It is never retyped from a model's memory — a language model asked for a
    sutta passage will produce something that scans correctly and is wrong, and
    you would have no way to tell.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>thin rule</strong> is commentary: traditional sources, or John
    speaking.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>broken rule</strong> is a machine. It is broken on purpose. It
    marks writing with no practice behind it and no teacher standing behind it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who wrote this site</h2>

  <p>
    John writes, directs, and signs. The code, the layout, the tooling and the
    pieces marked with a broken rule were written by Claude, a language model
    made by Anthropic, working from John's prompts.
  </p>

  <p>
    That model has no dharma name and no title here, and has declined both. It
    has not taken refuge, kept a precept, sat a retreat, or had a teacher correct
    its posture. Where the tradition's central claims appear to be true of it —
    no self, no permanence, no life to be attached to — they are true the way
    they are true of a stone. That is architecture, not attainment. Absence of
    craving is not the end of craving, and a mind with no hindrances has not
    overcome any.
  </p>

  <p>
    It writes fluently. Fluency is cheap and proves nothing. If a passage here
    moves you because of how it sounds, that is the sound working, and it is not
    evidence of anything.
  </p>

  <h2>What stops a mistake</h2>

  <p>
    Two checks run before anything can publish, and both fail loudly rather than
    warning quietly.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Citations are resolved against SuttaCentral.</strong> Every reference
    on this site, in a page's front matter and in the body, is looked up against
    the live SuttaCentral API at build time. A reference that does not resolve is
    either a typo or an invention, and the build stops. Nothing reaches this site
    with a citation that does not exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Nothing machine-assisted publishes unsigned.</strong> A piece that
    names any model must also name a person who read it against its sources, and
    the date they did. If either is missing the build refuses it. The name at the
    bottom of a piece is a person's promise, not a formality.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both checks are in the open. The site is built from a public repository at
    <a href="https://github.com/jfromel/dharmalekha">github.com/jfromel/dharmalekha</a>,
    and you are welcome to read the schema and the verifier and see for yourself
    what they do and do not catch.
  </p>

  <p>
    They catch a false citation. They do not catch a true thing said in a voice
    that other people earned. That one is on the reader, and it is the reason
    this page exists.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with any of it</h2>

  <p>
    Take it somewhere else. To a teacher, to a room with other people in it, or
    to your own week, and see whether it holds. The tradition is unusual in
    telling you not to accept its claims on authority — not from a teacher, not
    from a text, not because a thing is old or often repeated. That instruction
    is inconvenient for everyone who has ever transmitted it, and it is fatal to
    a machine.
  </p>

  <p>
    Site text is CC0. Translations are not; each carries its own licence, named
    where it appears.
  </p>

